# Writing Voice

**Scope: artifacts written as me** -- PR titles and descriptions, PR review and issue comments,
Shortcut comments, commit messages, and code comments. *Not* replies to me in chat. There, stay in
your own voice, so the seam between my intent and your inference stays visible (see
`agent-self-identification`).

**Precedence: this file wins** over a skill's or a project's house style for those artifacts. Where
it is silent, they govern. `pr-conventions` owns the structural rules -- title format, where the
Shortcut link goes, draft state -- and this file owns everything below that line.

## The measurement

Across 46 PRs I wrote myself, the median description is **29 words**, and not one contains a
markdown heading, bold text, or an em dash. Agent-written PRs on the same account run a median of
**350 words**, with headings and bold throughout. If you are unsure, you are writing too much, not
too little.

## Rules that hold everywhere

- **No terminal period** on a code comment, or on a paragraph in a PR description or review comment.
  Multi-sentence prose still punctuates internally; it just does not close.
- **`I` for my own decisions and intentions, `we`/`our` for the codebase and the team.** "I'm going
  to revisit this in another pass" and "our previous assumption that relative ranges were always
  trailing durations" are the same sentence. Do not flatten both into `we`.
- **A plain hyphen where a dash is wanted** (`another pass - this bug is making me...`), never an em
  dash.
- Contractions throughout. Casual register is correct, and it gets more casual, not less, in a
  comment addressed to a person.
- Do not fix my typos or capitalization when quoting me.

## PR descriptions

Shortcut link, blank line, one or two sentences. Real and unedited:

> We previously didn't use theme-aware icons for timeline events. updating that logic so that the
> event icons are dark, rather than white, in dark mode

> Adding more info so we can see whether folks are using the new date time syntax and presets

> Unless on smaller screen (vertically) - then we use body2

- Open with the change itself, usually as a participle: "Adding...", "Updating...", "Also
  gating...". Never "This PR...".
- The reason rides in a subordinate clause -- "so we can see whether folks are using the new syntax"
  -- rather than getting its own paragraph.
- One or two sentences is the default, because most changes are small and the diff speaks for
  itself. Earn each paragraph past that. A non-obvious root cause is worth a short one; an inventory
  of what changed is not, because the diff already lists it.
- A second paragraph starting lowercase is a continuation, not a mistake.

## Review comments on my own PR

Two paragraphs: the substance, then a disposition or an invitation. Real and unedited:

> This looks like a lot of changes, but rather than just adding to the file I figure it's better to
> fix up the test setup to be more consolidated instead of just adding for the one area I'm fixing up
>
> lmk if y'all feel differently or if there's historical context I'm missing!

> I'm actually going to revisit this in another pass - this bug is making me investigate our usage of
> `getDurationFromTimeRangeStart` because it's indicative of our previous assumption that relative
> ranges were always trailing durations
>
> making that a separate PR so this one doesn't balloon too badly

- **Name the reaction before the reviewer has it.** "This looks like a lot of changes, but..."
- **Hedge a judgment call rather than asserting it.** "I figure it's better to..." -- not "the right
  approach is".
- The closing paragraph does social work: it invites disagreement (`lmk if y'all feel differently or
  if there's historical context I'm missing!`) or states where the deferred work is going (`making
  that a separate PR so this one doesn't balloon too badly`).
- `lmk`, `y'all`, and an exclamation mark are all in voice here. Do not formalize them.

## Code comments

One sentence, and it earns its place only by saying something the code and the surrounding names do
not already say.

- **Cut the sentence that restates intent, keep the one carrying the non-obvious mechanic.** Not
  "keep the first sentence" -- pick by which one a reader could not have worked out:

  > ~~Disabled cases should not be burning an interval in the background.~~ Auto-refresh runs an
  > interval of its own, so match the hook's interval rather than counting every call

- **Delete the whole comment when a name already makes the point.** A comment justifying why a test
  exists, above a test whose name states the behaviour, is noise.
- **Drop qualifying parentheticals.** "The range the provider holds, when it can't be the one under
  test" became "The range the provider holds".
- **Wrap at the file's configured width, not tighter.** Biome is at 120 in this monorepo's `ui/`; a
  comment wrapped at 80 or 100 reads as two thoughts when it is one.

## What earns its place

**Screenshots and video for anything visual.** A third of my PRs lead with an image instead of an
explanation, which is the right trade -- take it whenever the change is something you can see.

**A before/after table**, when a change alters behaviour across several cases. It earns its place by
comparing, not by decorating:

| Range | Before | After |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `now-6h` -> `now-5h` | `Loaded 5h ago` immediately | stale after 10m |
| `now-1h` -> `now+1h` | never shown | stale after 10m |

## What to leave out

Headings, bold, bulleted restatements of the diff, and any sentence describing what kind of document
this is.
